WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.880
Least privilege means granting only the access needed for a role, task, service, or system function.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:07.780
It is not simply a slogan.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:18.080
It requires understanding real workflows, separating routine work from privileged operations, and designing permissions that are narrow enough to reduce risk but usable enough for operations.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:22.700
If access is too broad, compromise or mistakes have greater impact.

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:26.680
If access is too narrow without support, users find workarounds.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:29.460
Permission granularity is a design tradeoff.

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:34.400
Very coarse permissions are easy to administer but may overexpose sensitive operations.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:39.740
Extremely fine-grained permissions can become difficult to understand, test, and review.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:46.200
Default deny and allowlist thinking help: access should be denied unless an explicit rule or policy allows it.

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:50.580
This creates a safer baseline and forces teams to define intended access.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:56.400
Separation of duties reduces the chance that one identity can complete a high-risk workflow alone.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:05.260
Temporary access and just-in-time access concepts can reduce standing privilege by granting elevated permission only when needed and only for a limited period.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:11.320
Privilege review helps confirm that users, roles, groups, and service accounts still need the access they have.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:16.100
Permission design must also avoid role explosion and overbroad administrator roles.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:21.680
Every exception should not become a permanent role, and every support need should not become full administration.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:29.020
Strong designs use clear permission names, ownership, approval paths, review cadence, and operational guidance.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:33.560
Least privilege succeeds when it supports both security and practical work.
